# Bitwise Operations
Little-endian has the LSB stored in the lowest memory address; big-endian puts the MSB there. 

ASR copies the MSB into the holes. This is particularly useful for signed values.

Rotation is when the fallen off bits are cycled into the holes.

For unsigned, the *additive inverse* of a number `⊖(n, u)` is the number that generates a sum of 0 via unsigned carry (i.e., loops to 0 on the number wheel). In signed, `⊖(n, s)` is simply the negation. 0 and \[-2^{n-1}\] are themselves. `(~x) + 1` is the formula for finding the additive inverse. In subtraction, the additive inverse of the second argument is added to the first argument.

Signed overflow is if x and y have a different sign to the sum or difference.

Multiplication is iterated addition; however, strength reduction speeds this up by breaking the multiplier into sums of powers of 2. Use each exponent in a LSL then sum these results to get the final product.

Do `x + (1 << k) - 1` before dividing to get the ceiling `⎡x / 2^k⎤` of a signed quotient.